select public.prepify_load_bank('Chemistry', 9, $j$[["A student places water in a sealed flask. After some time, the water level remains unchanged although evaporation continues. This is because:",["Evaporation has stopped","Condensation has stopped","Evaporation and condensation occur at equal rates","Water molecules stop moving"],"C",5,1,"f680f9a0d219"],["A chemist observes that the concentration of reactants and products remain constant for several hours. Which conclusion is correct?",["The reaction has stopped","The reaction is irreversible","The system has reached equilibrium","All reactants are consumed"],"C",5,1,"5282434008ab"],["In a closed vessel, nitrogen and hydrogen react to form ammonia. Initially, ammonia forms rapidly. After some time, the rate of ammonia formation decreases because:",["Reverse reaction begins to occur","Catalyst is consumed","Temperature decreases automatically","Pressure becomes zero"],"A",5,1,"18e061bcf591"],["A student claims that no reaction occurs at equilibrium because concentrations remain constant. Why is the statement incorrect?",["Concentrations are not constant","Only reverse reaction occurs","Both forward and reverse reactions continue at equal rates","Products disappear completely"],"C",5,1,"321770fcf098"],["Hydrated copper(II) sulphate crystals are heated until they become white. This colour change indicates:",["Formation of copper metal","Removal of water molecules","Addition of oxygen","Formation of a new element"],"B",5,1,"0980a98b7ef7"],["A white sample of anhydrous copper(II) sulphate turns blue after exposure to moist air. This demonstrates:",["Dehydration","Oxidation","Reversible hydration","Neutralization"],"C",5,1,"9be398229eea"],["A pink cobalt(II) chloride sample is heated and becomes blue. The heating process favors:",["Hydration","Dehydration","Condensation","Dissolution"],"B",5,1,"3da85d8027e4"],["A sealed container contains liquid water and water vapour at equilibrium. If some vapour is removed, the system will:",["Remain unchanged","Produce more vapour through evaporation","Stop evaporating","Freeze immediately"],"B",5,1,"3de082adebf7"],["In an equilibrium system, a student observes millions of molecules reacting every second. This observation supports the concept of:",["Static equilibrium","Dynamic equilibrium","Irreversible change","Chemical inactivity"],"B",5,1,"e625043830d3"],["A reaction mixture at equilibrium contains 40% reactants and 60% products. Which statement is correct?",["Forward rate is greater than reverse rate","Reverse rate is greater than forward rate","Rates of both reactions are equal","Reaction has stopped"],"C",5,1,"a298037f183a"],["A student increases the temperature of an equilibrium system involving dehydration of hydrated copper sulphate. The equilibrium shifts toward:",["Hydrated salt","Water formation only","Anhydrous salt formation","No change"],"C",5,1,"9da52cffcb18"],["Why is a closed system necessary for establishing equilibrium?",["To prevent catalyst loss","To maintain colour changes","To prevent escape of reactants or products","To increase reaction speed only"],"C",5,1,"1178c4659b26"],["A reaction reaches equilibrium after 30 minutes. After another hour, concentrations remain unchanged. This means:",["No molecules react anymore","Forward and reverse reactions continue equally","Products have disappeared","Temperature is increasing continuously"],"B",5,1,"93bfd5ef3121"],["A student notices that opening the lid of a bottle containing an equilibrium mixture disturbs the equilibrium because:",["The system is no longer closed","Temperature becomes zero","Molecules stop moving","Reverse reaction becomes impossible"],"A",5,1,"c0549c12513a"],["A sample of hydrated cobalt chloride is left in a dry environment and gradually turns blue. This happens because:",["It absorbs water","It loses water molecules","It forms cobalt metal","It reacts with oxygen only"],"B",5,1,"7414cac8e072"],["Which observation best indicates equilibrium has been reached?",["Reactants disappear completely","Product concentration becomes zero","Measurable properties remain constant with time","Colour changes continuously"],"C",5,1,"250ede59f20b"],["In a reversible reaction, the forward reaction rate initially decreases because:",["Product concentration increases","Catalyst is destroyed","Temperature becomes zero","Molecules stop colliding"],"A",5,1,"2f1d03704789"],["A student says equilibrium means equal amounts of reactants and products. The correct explanation is:",["Always true","True only in physical changes","Equilibrium requires equal rates, not necessarily equal amounts","Reactants must be absent"],"C",5,1,"b04ba1339873"],["When water vapour condenses inside a sealed flask, the process represents:",["Forward reaction only","Reverse process of evaporation","Decomposition reaction","Neutralization"],"B",5,1,"61dd7e76adbd"],["The blue colour of hydrated copper sulphate reappears when:",["Water is added to the white anhydrous salt","The salt is heated strongly","Oxygen is removed","Copper is extracted"],"A",5,1,"6b1db0a43f39"],["Which situation best represents dynamic equilibrium?",["A stopped pendulum","Water continuously evaporating from an open dish","Liquid water and vapour in a sealed bottle","A burnt matchstick"],"C",5,1,"0ad0951ee82d"],["In a reaction at equilibrium, increasing the concentration of a reactant generally:",["Disturbs equilibrium","Stops both reactions","Makes the reaction irreversible","Removes products instantly"],"A",5,1,"fc613f252475"],["A scientist records equal forward and reverse reaction rates. The system must:",["Be at equilibrium","Have no products present","Be irreversible","Have stopped reacting"],"A",5,1,"b34c90304d4a"],["A reversible reaction reaches equilibrium faster when:",["Molecules collide more effectively","Container is opened","Products are removed continuously","Reactants are absent"],"A",5,1,"11093b2d0042"],["Which change clearly demonstrates a reversible reaction?",["Burning magnesium ribbon","Hydration and dehydration of copper sulphate","Rusting iron","Digestion of food"],"B",5,1,"c30622fc45b1"],["A student heats hydrated cobalt chloride and observes a colour change. Which evidence suggests equilibrium has shifted?",["Change in mass only","Change in colour only","Formation of a new element","Explosion occurs"],"B",5,1,"59320645da40"],["A student heats blue hydrated copper sulphate repeatedly and then adds water. The colour alternates between blue and white. This demonstrates:",["Irreversible change","Reversible change","Oxidation only","Reduction only"],"B",5,1,"fc0333acd62d"],["A reaction is at equilibrium. Suddenly the forward rate becomes greater than the reverse rate. This means:",["Equilibrium is disturbed","Equilibrium remains unchanged","Reaction has stopped","Reverse reaction disappeared permanently"],"A",5,1,"95b85680362a"],["A reaction vessel at equilibrium is shaken thoroughly. Assuming no temperature change, equilibrium:",["Is permanently destroyed","Remains established","Becomes irreversible","Eliminates products"],"B",5,1,"263d30f22a29"],["The strongest evidence for dynamic equilibrium is that:",["Reactants disappear completely","Products stop forming","Opposing reactions continue at equal rates","The container becomes empty"],"C",5,1,"636bba8b002d"],["A reaction in which products can reform the original reactants is called:",["Combination reaction","Irreversible reaction","Reversible reaction","Decomposition reaction"],"C",1,0,"52d0b6d59097"],["Which symbol is used to represent a reversible reaction?",["→","←","⇌","="],"C",1,0,"dd38c2e7f302"],["In a reversible reaction, the reaction proceeds:",["Only in forward direction","Only in reverse direction","In both forward and reverse directions","In neither direction"],"C",1,0,"69d7962a0e1a"],["Chemical equilibrium is achieved when:",["Reactants are completely consumed","Products stop forming","Forward and reverse reaction rates become equal","Reaction stops completely"],"C",1,0,"313d9005e76a"],["Chemical equilibrium can be established only in:",["Open system","Closed system","Isolated system only","Any container"],"B",1,0,"76cd5e802029"],["In a closed container, liquid and vapour reach equilibrium when:",["Evaporation stops","Condensation stops","Evaporation and condensation occur at equal rates","All liquid evaporates"],"C",1,0,"993d2a22e002"],["At equilibrium, the concentration of reactants and products:",["Continually increases","Continually decreases","Remains constant","Becomes zero"],"C",1,0,"3d0fa1e83f60"],["Which reaction is an example of a reversible reaction?",["Burning of paper","Rusting of iron","N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃","Explosion of firecrackers"],"C",1,0,"56bce61a84c3"],["The conversion of reactants into products is called:",["Reverse reaction","Forward reaction","Neutralization","Condensation"],"B",1,0,"4b96ba4d3a30"],["The conversion of products back into reactants is called:",["Forward reaction","Combination reaction","Reverse reaction","Oxidation reaction"],"C",1,0,"e9ffaffd4aa1"],["At the beginning of a reversible reaction, the reverse reaction rate is often:",["Maximum","Equal to forward rate","Zero","Constant"],"C",1,0,"48799ea0bc6f"],["In the reaction 2SO₂ + O₂ ⇌ 2SO₃, SO₃ acts as:",["Reactant in forward reaction","Product in forward reaction","Catalyst","Solvent"],"B",1,0,"859adaf84b68"],["Dynamic equilibrium means:",["Reaction stops completely","Only forward reaction continues","Both reactions continue at equal rates","Only reverse reaction continues"],"C",1,0,"424e9cedc13f"],["Which condition is necessary for equilibrium?",["Constant temperature","Constant concentration","Constant pressure or volume","All of these"],"D",1,0,"7fae18741867"],["If temperature changes, equilibrium may:",["Remain unaffected","Shift to a new position","Stop permanently","Become irreversible"],"B",1,0,"73edd804a8fb"],["When hydrated copper(II) sulphate is heated, its colour changes from:",["White to blue","Pink to blue","Blue to white","Blue to pink"],"C",1,1,"14c81b10d8ca"],["Hydrated copper(II) sulphate is:",["White solid","Blue solid","Pink solid","Green solid"],"B",1,0,"bda152ed0839"],["Anhydrous copper(II) sulphate is:",["Blue solid","Green solid","White solid","Pink solid"],"C",1,0,"ac736ee45131"],["Adding water to anhydrous copper(II) sulphate causes it to become:",["Black","White","Green","Blue"],"D",1,0,"3c8a61bfe167"],["Heating hydrated copper(II) sulphate removes:",["Sulphur dioxide","Oxygen","Water of crystallization","Copper ions"],"C",1,0,"1dcdaad6c607"],["Hydrated cobalt(II) chloride is:",["Blue solid","Pink solid","White solid","Yellow solid"],"B",1,0,"3048a70470fb"],["Anhydrous cobalt(II) chloride is:",["Pink solid","Blue solid","Green solid","White solid"],"B",1,0,"1a7e13ce5e76"],["When hydrated cobalt(II) chloride is heated, it:",["Turns pink","Turns blue","Turns green","Melts"],"B",1,1,"a3de346ecc97"],["Adding water to anhydrous cobalt(II) chloride shifts equilibrium:",["To the right","To the left","Upward","Downward"],"B",1,0,"72e4b7559be3"],["Which reaction is favored when temperature increases?",["Exothermic reaction","Endothermic reaction","Neutralization reaction","Combustion reaction"],"B",1,0,"c4d560bb66ce"],["At equilibrium, the amount of reactants:",["Continuously decreases","Continuously increases","Remains constant","Becomes zero"],"C",1,0,"b61a748c45b7"],["Which statement is true at equilibrium?",["No molecules react","Only products are present","Reaction rates are equal","Reactants disappear completely"],"C",1,0,"11f621f8d1da"],["A reaction that goes to completion is called:",["Reversible reaction","Equilibrium reaction","Irreversible reaction","Dynamic reaction"],"C",1,0,"d4168272b1f5"],["Equilibrium can be achieved when:",["Only reactants are placed in a closed container","Only products are placed in a closed container","Reactants and products are placed together","Any of these situations"],"D",1,0,"11cc4c00c0cb"],["Which process occurs simultaneously in a reversible reaction?",["Melting and freezing","Forward and reverse reactions","Oxidation only","Reduction only"],"B",1,0,"4d7bbfc341f1"],["Sulphur trioxide can decompose into:",["SO₂ and O₂","S and O₂","SO₂ only","SO₄ only"],"A",1,0,"6282c4f35524"],["Which of the following is NOT true for equilibrium?",["Forward rate equals reverse rate","Concentrations remain constant","Reaction stops completely","Dynamic condition exists"],"C",1,0,"6b092ee2bcf4"],["If the evaporation rate becomes equal to the condensation rate, the system is:",["Melting","Boiling","At equilibrium","Freezing"],"C",1,0,"7f2bbe2112e9"],["Equilibrium is established in an irreversible reaction:",["Quickly","Slowly","Never","At high temperature only"],"C",1,0,"949ef77c5a5a"],["In a closed bottle of water, the rate of evaporation becomes equal to the rate of condensation. The system has reached:",["melting","boiling","equilibrium","freezing"],"C",1,1,"7f2bbe2112e9"],["Blue anhydrous cobalt(II) chloride paper is dipped in water. It turns:",["pink","green","white","black"],"A",3,0,"a3de346ecc97"],["White anhydrous copper sulphate turns blue when a liquid is added to it. This shows the liquid contains:",["water","oxygen","copper","salt"],"A",3,1,"6b1db0a43f39"],["At dynamic equilibrium:",["both reactions stop","the forward and reverse reactions continue at equal rates","only products are present","only the forward reaction occurs"],"B",3,0,"321770fcf098"],["Heating blue copper sulphate crystals turns them white because they lose:",["sulphur","oxygen","water of crystallization","copper"],"C",1,0,"1dcdaad6c607"],["A reaction mixture in a closed flask keeps the same colour and pressure for an hour. This suggests that:",["the reaction has reached equilibrium","all reactants are used up","the reaction is irreversible","no products formed"],"A",3,1,"250ede59f20b"],["For H₂ + I₂ ⇌ 2HI at equilibrium, [H₂] = [I₂] = 0.1 mol/dm³ and [HI] = 0.5 mol/dm³. The value of Kc is:",["25","0.04","50","1.25"],"A",5,0,"gen-chemistry-c9-kc-value"],["For H₂ + I₂ ⇌ 2HI at equilibrium, [H₂] = [I₂] = 0.2 mol/dm³ and [HI] = 0.8 mol/dm³. The value of Kc is:",["16","20","1.6","0.0625"],"A",5,0,"gen-chemistry-c9-kc-value"],["For H₂ + I₂ ⇌ 2HI at equilibrium, [H₂] = [I₂] = 0.1 mol/dm³ and [HI] = 0.3 mol/dm³. The value of Kc is:",["0.1111","0.45","30","9"],"D",5,0,"gen-chemistry-c9-kc-value"],["For H₂ + I₂ ⇌ 2HI at equilibrium, [H₂] = [I₂] = 0.5 mol/dm³ and [HI] = 1 mol/dm³. The value of Kc is:",["4","1","0.25","8"],"A",5,0,"gen-chemistry-c9-kc-value"],["For H₂ + I₂ ⇌ 2HI at equilibrium, [H₂] = [I₂] = 0.1 mol/dm³ and [HI] = 0.9 mol/dm³. The value of Kc is:",["81","4.05","0.0123","90"],"A",5,0,"gen-chemistry-c9-kc-value"],["For H₂ + I₂ ⇌ 2HI at equilibrium, [H₂] = [I₂] = 0.1 mol/dm³ and [HI] = 0.7 mol/dm³. The value of Kc is:",["2.45","0.0204","70","49"],"D",5,0,"gen-chemistry-c9-kc-value"],["The equilibrium constant expression (Kc) for N₂ + 3H₂ ⇌ 2NH₃ is:",["([N₂][H₂]³) / [NH₃]²","2[NH₃] / ([N₂] 3[H₂])","[NH₃] / ([N₂][H₂])","[NH₃]² / ([N₂][H₂]³)"],"D",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c9-kc-expression"],["The equilibrium constant expression (Kc) for PCl₅ ⇌ PCl₃ + Cl₂ is:",["[PCl₅] / ([PCl₃][Cl₂])","[PCl₃] / [PCl₅]","[PCl₃][Cl₂] / [PCl₅]","([PCl₃] + [Cl₂]) / [PCl₅]"],"C",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c9-kc-expression"],["The equilibrium constant expression (Kc) for 2SO₂ + O₂ ⇌ 2SO₃ is:",["([SO₂]²[O₂]) / [SO₃]²","[SO₃]² / ([SO₂]²[O₂])","[SO₃]² / ([SO₂][O₂])","[SO₃] / ([SO₂][O₂])"],"B",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c9-kc-expression"],["The equilibrium constant expression (Kc) for H₂ + I₂ ⇌ 2HI is:",["2[HI] / ([H₂][I₂])","[HI]² / ([H₂][I₂])","([H₂][I₂]) / [HI]²","[HI] / ([H₂][I₂])"],"B",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c9-kc-expression"],["The equilibrium constant expression (Kc) for 2NO₂ ⇌ N₂O₄ is:",["[N₂O₄] / [NO₂]²","[NO₂]² / [N₂O₄]","[N₂O₄] / 2[NO₂]","[N₂O₄] / [NO₂]"],"A",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c9-kc-expression"],["The equilibrium constant expression (Kc) for N₂ + O₂ ⇌ 2NO is:",["[NO]² / ([N₂][O₂])","([N₂][O₂]) / [NO]²","[NO] / ([N₂][O₂])","2[NO] / ([N₂][O₂])"],"A",3,0,"gen-chemistry-c9-kc-expression"]]$j$::jsonb, true);
select public.prepify_load_bank('Chemistry', 10, $j$[["According to Arrhenius theory, an acid produces which ion in water?",["OH⁻","H⁺","Na⁺","Cl⁻"],"B",1,0,"aea33845af73"],["According to Arrhenius theory, a base produces which ion in water?",["H⁺","Cl⁻","OH⁻","Na⁺"],"C",1,0,"55b97a874435"],["Which scientist proposed the Arrhenius theory of acids and bases?",["Dalton","Rutherford","Arrhenius","Bohr"],"C",1,0,"0a98084869a8"],["Acids generally have a ______ taste.",["Bitter","Sweet","Sour","Salty"],"C",1,0,"8c7b33b3cb93"],["Bases generally have a ______ taste.",["Sour","Bitter","Sweet","Salty"],"B",1,0,"3a6204b06fbe"],["An acid turns blue litmus paper:",["Green","Yellow","Red","Black"],"C",1,0,"e7b76ec5e747"],["A base turns red litmus paper:",["Blue","Green","Yellow","Red"],"A",1,0,"6df65bbaaf9e"],["Which of the following is a common acid?",["NaOH","KOH","HCl","Ca(OH)₂"],"C",1,0,"bf23faf0a3b3"],["Which of the following is a common base?",["HNO₃","H₂SO₄","H₃PO₄","NaOH"],"D",1,0,"a613d04fa83d"],["Sodium hydroxide is commonly used in:",["Soap making","Photography","Food preservation","Medicine"],"A",1,0,"5e9800913c27"],["Potassium hydroxide is used in making:",["Cement","Liquid soap","Fertilizers","Paints"],"B",1,0,"3373069abcb8"],["Calcium hydroxide is used in making:",["Mortar and cement","Explosives","Soft drinks","Perfumes"],"A",1,0,"18f27da6b76f"],["Magnesium hydroxide is commonly used as:",["Fuel","Fertilizer","Antacid","Dye"],"C",1,0,"d4d0a398a50a"],["A Bronsted-Lowry acid is a:",["Proton acceptor","Electron donor","Proton donor","Salt former"],"C",1,0,"854ed33e513c"],["A Bronsted-Lowry base is a:",["Proton acceptor","Proton donor","Metal oxide","Salt"],"A",1,0,"f9a005a997da"],["In the reaction HCl + H₂O → H₃O⁺ + Cl⁻, HCl acts as:",["Base","Acid","Salt","Catalyst"],"B",1,0,"09b6300d3ba9"],["In the reaction HCl + H₂O → H₃O⁺ + Cl⁻, water acts as:",["Acid","Salt","Base","Metal"],"C",1,0,"5940a032fba7"],["In the reaction H₂O + NH₃ → NH₄⁺ + OH⁻, NH₃ acts as:",["Acid","Base","Salt","Indicator"],"B",1,0,"6716ac777562"],["In the reaction H₂O + NH₃ → NH₄⁺ + OH⁻, water acts as:",["Acid","Base","Salt","Alkali"],"A",1,0,"cf754ef8d63e"],["Water can behave as both an acid and a base. Such substances are called:",["Neutral","Amphoteric","Basic","Acidic"],"B",1,0,"26d67d81ebf6"],["Which of the following is an amphoteric substance?",["HCl","NaOH","H₂O","HNO₃"],"C",1,0,"98311d98120b"],["Which acid is used in the manufacture of fertilizers and explosives?",["HCl","H₃PO₄","HNO₃","CH₃COOH"],"C",1,0,"e4f883e789ef"],["Hydrochloric acid is commonly used for:",["Cleaning metals","Making cement","Food preservation","Photography"],"A",1,0,"0ef510038e10"],["Acids and bases in aqueous solutions can:",["Conduct electricity","Produce light","Float on water","Become gases"],"A",1,0,"b582ceb672ac"],["Which ion is responsible for acidic properties?",["Na⁺","OH⁻","H⁺","Cl⁻"],"C",1,0,"ecf7537ba54f"],["Which ion is responsible for basic properties?",["H⁺","OH⁻","NO₃⁻","SO₄²⁻"],"B",1,0,"d617d5f8bd2a"],["NaOH dissociates in water to form:",["H⁺ and OH⁻","Na⁺ and OH⁻","Na⁺ and Cl⁻","H⁺ and Cl⁻"],"B",1,0,"de87d8d59088"],["HCl dissociates in water to form:",["H⁺ and Cl⁻","H⁺ and OH⁻","Na⁺ and Cl⁻","K⁺ and OH⁻"],"A",1,0,"8d2a1d9b6366"],["Which of the following is a base?",["H₂SO₄","HNO₃","NH₄OH","HCl"],"C",1,0,"d7113c40c0d0"],["Which of the following is an acid?",["KOH","NaOH","Ca(OH)₂","H₃PO₄"],"D",1,0,"506d57c5a4a5"],["Which acid is used as an acidulant in food?",["HCl","HNO₃","H₂SO₄","H₃PO₄"],"D",1,0,"509fbac5135a"],["Bases that are soluble in water are called:",["Acids","Alkalis","Salts","Oxides"],"B",1,0,"d606714e0593"],["Which of the following is an alkali?",["NaOH","CuO","CO₂","SO₂"],"A",1,0,"95b6025ea2e1"],["A substance that accepts a proton forms a:",["Negative ion","Positive ion","Neutral atom","Molecule only"],"B",1,0,"ce6d6a5c1a83"],["After donating a proton, an acid usually forms a:",["Positive ion","Neutral atom","Negative ion","Molecule"],"C",1,1,"35b13ebb99ce"],["Which of the following is NOT a property of acids?",["Sour taste","Turn blue litmus red","Produce H⁺ ions in water","Turn red litmus blue"],"D",1,0,"4498d33c4c0d"],["Which of the following is NOT a property of bases?",["Bitter taste","Produce OH⁻ ions in water","Turn red litmus blue","Turn blue litmus red"],"D",1,0,"5a6e38eaf26a"],["NH₃ behaves as a base because it:",["Donates electrons","Accepts a proton","Produces Cl⁻ ions","Produces H⁺ ions directly"],"B",1,0,"e0991390928f"],["Which acid is commonly present in laboratory cleaning solutions?",["Hydrochloric acid","Sodium hydroxide","Potassium hydroxide","Calcium hydroxide"],"A",1,0,"f551ef00a27c"],["The Bronsted-Lowry theory defines acids and bases in terms of:",["Electrons","Neutrons","Protons","Atoms"],"C",1,0,"06f9e6bcefd7"],["Which pair correctly represents an acid and a base respectively?",["HCl and NaOH","NaOH and HNO₃","KOH and H₂SO₄","NH₄OH and HCl"],"A",1,0,"b3306c352893"],["Which of the following acids is used in the manufacture of many chemicals, dyes, and paints?",["HCl","H₂SO₄","HNO₃","H₃PO₄"],"B",1,0,"fa5030b0cdf8"],["The study of acids, bases, and their reactions helps explain:",["Only physical changes","Only nuclear reactions","Many everyday chemical processes","Only biological reactions"],"C",1,0,"ac3faddc3d6c"],["A student places blue litmus paper into an unknown solution. The paper immediately turns red. The solution most likely contains:",["OH⁻ ions","H⁺ ions","Na⁺ ions","Cl⁻ ions"],"B",5,1,"e69b66a681c2"],["During an experiment, a solution changes red litmus paper to blue. According to Arrhenius theory, the solution contains a high concentration of:",["H⁺ ions","H₃O⁺ ions","OH⁻ ions","Cl⁻ ions"],"C",5,1,"c9a9c493d4d9"],["A factory uses a chemical to remove rust from iron surfaces. Which acid is most likely being used?",["H₃PO₄","HCl","NH₄OH","Mg(OH)₂"],"B",5,1,"ea8033554c80"],["A patient complains of stomach acidity. The doctor recommends milk of magnesia. This substance works because it is a:",["Strong acid","Salt","Base","Metal oxide"],"C",5,1,"5852f2912f75"],["A chemist adds ammonia to water. The ammonia molecule accepts a proton from water. According to Bronsted-Lowry theory, ammonia behaves as a:",["Salt","Acid","Base","Catalyst"],"C",5,1,"ef8e770b94ce"],["A student accidentally spills a solution on red litmus paper, but no color change occurs. Which statement is most likely correct?",["The solution is acidic","The solution is basic","The solution may be neutral","The solution contains only H⁺ ions"],"C",5,1,"266facde7f2c"],["A farmer applies a chemical containing phosphoric acid to improve crop growth. The acid is mainly used in the manufacture of:",["Detergents","Fertilizers","Plastics","Dyes"],"B",5,1,"a15bac98cd8e"],["In the reaction HCl + H₂O → H₃O⁺ + Cl⁻, the water molecule acts as a proton:",["Donor","Acceptor","Producer","Neutralizer"],"B",5,1,"28c21a3723d4"],["A student observes that a substance has a bitter taste and turns red litmus blue. The substance is most likely:",["An acid","A salt","A base","A metal"],"C",5,1,"fcc04fc352d7"],["During an industrial process, nitric acid is selected because of its role in producing fertilizers and explosives. Its chemical formula is:",["H₂SO₄","HCl","HNO₃","H₃PO₄"],"C",5,1,"8d030c4af61c"],["A solution contains a large number of OH⁻ ions. Which observation would be expected?",["Blue litmus turns red","Red litmus turns blue","Sour taste","Release of H⁺ ions"],"B",5,1,"429af6aae8d8"],["A chemist identifies a substance as amphoteric. Which behavior confirms this property?",["It reacts only as an acid","It reacts only as a base","It can act as both an acid and a base","It does not react at all"],"C",5,1,"727692984e8a"],["A laboratory technician is preparing liquid soap. Which base would be most suitable?",["Ca(OH)₂","Mg(OH)₂","KOH","HCl"],"C",5,1,"27af15436335"],["A student is given four unlabeled bottles containing HCl, HNO₃, NaOH, and KOH. Which two substances will turn red litmus paper blue?",["HCl and HNO₃","HCl and KOH","NaOH and KOH","HNO₃ and NaOH"],"C",5,1,"6ab2846d1612"],["A chemical engineer needs a compound for making mortar. Which substance should be selected?",["HNO₃","HCl","Ca(OH)₂","NH₄OH"],"C",5,1,"60bcc39ac387"],["A student claims that all proton donors are bases. Which observation disproves the claim?",["HCl donates a proton and acts as an acid","NH₃ accepts a proton","NaOH releases OH⁻ ions","Water is neutral"],"A",5,1,"027567cdd13b"],["A sample solution conducts electricity and turns blue litmus red. The solution most likely contains:",["H⁺ ions","OH⁻ ions","Na⁺ ions only","Neutral molecules only"],"A",5,1,"887c3be7310d"],["During a reaction, a molecule gains a proton. According to Bronsted-Lowry theory, that molecule is acting as a:",["Acid","Salt","Base","Metal"],"C",5,1,"108fc271b2ca"],["A scientist is studying substances that release H⁺ ions in water. Which theory is being applied?",["Atomic theory","Arrhenius theory","Kinetic theory","Molecular theory"],"B",5,1,"2721b68ba2a1"],["A bottle is labeled \"alkali.\" Which property must the substance possess?",["Soluble base","Insoluble base","Strong acid","Neutral salt"],"A",5,1,"1c7411f0a5e0"],["While studying acids, a student finds a substance used in food and soft drinks. The substance is most likely:",["HCl","H₂SO₄","H₃PO₄","NaOH"],"C",5,1,"09f839e8081d"],["A solution causes skin irritation and has a sour taste. Which group does it belong to?",["Bases","Acids","Salts","Metals"],"B",5,1,"28978e523e9b"],["A student wants to verify whether a solution is basic. Which indicator result would confirm this?",["Blue litmus becomes red","Red litmus becomes blue","Red litmus remains red in all cases","Blue litmus becomes colorless"],"B",5,1,"83f08c66da2a"],["In a reaction, NH₃ becomes NH₄⁺. This indicates that NH₃ has:",["Lost a proton","Gained a proton","Lost an electron only","Gained a neutron"],"B",5,1,"dea26f0c1a61"],["A technician handling laboratory chemicals notices that a solution contains H⁺ ions but no OH⁻ ions. The solution is most likely:",["Acidic","Basic","Neutral","Amphoteric"],"A",5,1,"6f7c6110974a"],["Which substance can behave as both proton donor and proton acceptor depending on conditions?",["HCl","NaOH","H₂O","HNO₃"],"C",5,1,"af24b9c1e030"],["Which pair consists entirely of bases?",["HCl and HNO₃","H₂SO₄ and H₃PO₄","NaOH and KOH","HCl and NaOH"],"C",5,1,"7bd3a49980b2"],["A substance accepts H⁺ from another substance and forms a positive ion. It is acting as a:",["Salt","Acid","Base","Catalyst"],"C",5,1,"648f95ea504f"],["Which observation indicates the presence of an alkali rather than merely a base?",["It reacts with acids","It contains OH⁻ ions","It is soluble in water","It turns red litmus blue"],"C",5,1,"6b92510ba6df"],["A chemical sample acts as an acid in one reaction and as a base in another. The sample is:",["Neutral","Amphoteric","Metallic","Inert"],"B",5,1,"5304b1c9a504"],["A chemistry student concludes that a solution is basic because it contains excess OH⁻ ions and changes indicator color accordingly. Which indicator observation supports this conclusion?",["Blue litmus → Red","Red litmus → Blue","Blue litmus → White","Red litmus → Yellow"],"B",5,1,"2765507e3e4e"],["An unknown oxide reacts with water to produce a solution that turns red litmus blue. The oxide is most likely:",["Acidic oxide","Neutral oxide","Basic oxide","Amphoteric oxide"],"C",5,1,"d742f84a580b"],["Magnesium oxide is added to water. The resulting solution becomes slightly basic. This occurs because magnesium oxide is a:",["Non-metal oxide","Acidic oxide","Basic oxide","Neutral oxide"],"C",5,1,"19c9de014b9b"],["When calcium oxide is mixed with water, the resulting solution turns red litmus blue. Which ion is mainly responsible for this behavior?",["H⁺","Cl⁻","OH⁻","Ca²⁺"],"C",5,1,"d8d1fdd1cf26"],["A student adds dilute HCl to magnesium ribbon. Gas bubbles are produced. The gas evolved is:",["Oxygen","Carbon dioxide","Hydrogen","Chlorine"],"C",5,1,"2338d3da